"An artifact promotion diagram traces one immutable release artifact through registries and deployment environments with explicit qualification gates.\n\nUse when: Verify that tested and deployed environments use the same artifact. Explain approval and evidence required for release promotion.\n\nAvoid when: The question is the internal job ordering of a build workflow.\n\nVisual grammar: Artifact nodes carry immutable digest or release identity. Environment nodes show where that artifact is installed or approved. Promotion edges name validation evidence and required authorization.\n\nBuild: Select the release artifact and record its immutable identity. Draw registry and environment destinations separately. Attach gate evidence and the actual promotion result to each transition. Route rollback references outside the main promotion line and preserve readable environment titles.\n\nCheck: Promotion preserves identity unless an explicit rebuild is shown. Mutable tags are not treated as immutable evidence. Approved, deployed, and healthy states remain distinct. Gate notes and digest labels are never crossed by arrows.\n\nPitfalls: Rebuilding between environments while implying identical tested bits. Claiming promotion completed from an approval alone.\n\nExample: One immutable image digest is validated in Test and then promoted unchanged into Production through an evidenced gate.\n\nAlternatives: CI/CD pipeline diagram, Software supply-chain provenance graph."
